  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-8x+12\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/d35f232f4a0ff7dc26238a1ac75ec8c2dc7216efa75f767dbbbac271b8a23a79.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2<x<6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
